-- Supplying arguments by name, and supplying a section binder.
-- Reference: doc/reference/functions/WITH.md

GIVEN principal IS A NUMBER
      rate      IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`interest on` principal `at` rate MEANS principal TIMES rate

-- The same call, positionally and by name.
#EVAL `interest on` 1000 `at` 0.05                            -- 50
#EVAL `interest on` WITH rate IS 0.05, principal IS 1000       -- 50

§ `Rates`
    GIVEN `the rate` IS A NUMBER

GIVETH A NUMBER
doubled MEANS `the rate` TIMES 2

-- `quadrupled` never mentions `the rate`. It reads it through `doubled`.
GIVETH A NUMBER
quadrupled MEANS doubled TIMES 2

#EVAL doubled    WITH `the rate` IS 5                          -- 10
#EVAL quadrupled WITH `the rate` IS 5                          -- 20

-- An override for one call and everything under it. Elsewhere the binder is
-- unchanged, which is what the second directive below shows.
GIVETH A NUMBER
`under the strict reading` MEANS quadrupled WITH `the rate` IS 10

#EVAL `under the strict reading`                               -- 40
#EVAL quadrupled WITH `the rate` IS 5                          -- 20

-- A rule with its own parameter as well: the site names both, because a call
-- is all-named or all-positional.
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
bump n MEANS n TIMES `the rate`

#EVAL bump WITH n IS 3, `the rate` IS 5                        -- 15
